# Chapter 4 · Marxist and Socialist Feminism

> Marxist feminists say women's oppression comes from **capitalism and private property**. Socialist feminists agree that capitalism matters, but add that **patriarchy** is a second, separate system, and that the two work together. The 2021 question asked for "the development of Marxist/Socialist feminism and the philosophical stance it holds on gender issues and exploitation", and the 2016 question asked how socialist feminism differs from radical feminism. This chapter answers both.

## 4.1  A few words from Marxism first

You cannot understand this school without five words from **Karl Marx** (1818–1883).

::: table | Five Marxist terms you need before Marxist feminism.
@widths 3.6,10.4
Term | Simple meaning
Capitalism | An economy in which a few **own the means of production** (land, factories, machines) and most people sell their **labour** for wages.
Class | A group defined by its place in production: the **bourgeoisie** (owners) and the **proletariat** (workers).
Exploitation | Workers produce more value than they are paid; the owner keeps the difference as **profit** (surplus value).
Base and superstructure | The **economy** (base) shapes law, family, religion and ideas (superstructure).
Reserve army of labour | A pool of unemployed or part-time workers who can be hired when needed and dropped when not.
:::

## 4.2  Marxist feminism: the founders

::: thinker Friedrich Engels | *The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State* (1884)
Engels argued that in early societies property was shared and descent was often traced through the mother. When men began to own **private property** (herds, land), they wanted to pass it on to **their own** sons. To be sure of paternity, they imposed **monogamy on women** and took control of the household. He called this "**the world historical defeat of the female sex**".
-> The conclusion: women's oppression began with **private property**, and will end when private property ends and women enter paid production on equal terms. Engels drew on the anthropologist **Lewis Henry Morgan** (*Ancient Society*, ==1877==).
:::

- **August Bebel** (*Woman and Socialism*, ==1879==) — a German socialist leader — argued that women and workers share a common enemy.
- **Clara Zetkin** (Germany) organised socialist women. At the international socialist women's conference in **Copenhagen** in ==1910== she proposed an annual **International Women's Day**, first held in ==1911==. The UN began observing it on ==8 March== in ==1975==.
- **Alexandra Kollontai** in the Soviet Union (after ==1917==) pushed for communal kitchens, childcare and easy divorce, so that women would be freed from domestic slavery.

::: define Marxist feminism | the school of class and property
The view that women's oppression is rooted in **capitalism and private property**. Women are exploited as **unpaid domestic workers** who reproduce the labour force, and as a **cheap, flexible reserve army** of paid labour. Gender oppression is ultimately a form of **class** oppression, and will end with the end of capitalism.
-> Key words: private property, class, exploitation, domestic labour, reproduction of labour power, reserve army.
:::

## 4.3  The domestic-labour debate (1969–1975)

Classic Marxism studied paid work in factories. Second-wave Marxist feminists asked: **what about the work women do at home?**

- **Margaret Benston** ("The Political Economy of Women's Liberation", ==1969==) argued that housework is real **production** — of meals, clean clothes and cared-for children — but because it is unpaid it is not counted as "work".
- **Mariarosa Dalla Costa and Selma James** (*The Power of Women and the Subversion of the Community*, ==1972==) argued that housework produces the most important commodity of all: **the worker**. Every morning the housewife sends a fed, rested worker to the factory. Capitalism profits from her unpaid labour.
- This led to the **Wages for Housework** campaign, launched in Italy in ==1972==. **Silvia Federici** ("Wages Against Housework", ==1975==) wrote: "They say it is love. We say it is unwaged work."

::: define Social reproduction | a key idea of the school
All the daily and generational work that **keeps people alive and able to work**: cooking, cleaning, caring for children, the sick and the old. Capitalism depends on it but does not pay for it.
-> Later writers (Lise Vogel, ==1983==; Tithi Bhattacharya, ==2017==) call this **social reproduction theory**.
:::

::: fig cycle | How women's unpaid work at home keeps capitalism running (the Marxist-feminist view).
@centre Capitalist profit
Housework: meals, rest and clean clothes for the worker
Childcare: raising the next generation of workers
Care of the sick and old: at no cost to the state
The worker goes to work: fit to produce value
Wages go to the "breadwinner": women depend on him
Cheap female labour: hired when needed, dropped when not
:::

## 4.4  From Marxist to socialist feminism

By the late 1970s many feminists felt that Marxism was **"sex-blind"**. It explained why *workers* are exploited, but not why *women* — in every class — are subordinate to *men*. Why do male workers also dominate their wives? Why did women in socialist countries still do all the housework?

::: thinker Juliet Mitchell | "Women: The Longest Revolution" (1966); *Woman's Estate* (1971)
Mitchell said women's position is shaped by **four structures**, not only the economy: **production**, **reproduction**, **sexuality** and the **socialisation of children**. Change in one alone will not free women.
-> A bridge from Marxist to socialist feminism. (She also defended psychoanalysis — Chapter 5.)
:::

::: thinker Heidi Hartmann | "The Unhappy Marriage of Marxism and Feminism" (1979)
Hartmann compared the union of Marxism and feminism to a marriage in English common law, where "husband and wife are one, and that one is the husband": Marxism had swallowed feminism. She called for a "healthier marriage" and a "divorce" if needed. Her **dual-systems theory** says **capitalism and patriarchy are two separate but linked systems**. For example, male-dominated trade unions fought for a **"family wage"** for men, which pushed women back into the home — capitalism and patriarchy working together.
-> Hartmann is the name to quote for socialist feminism.
:::

- **Zillah Eisenstein** (ed., *Capitalist Patriarchy and the Case for Socialist Feminism*, ==1979==) coined the term **capitalist patriarchy** for the single combined system.
- **Iris Marion Young** ("Beyond the Unhappy Marriage", ==1980==) argued for a **unified-systems** theory: capitalism was patriarchal from the start, because it relied on a gendered division of labour.
- **Maria Mies** (*Patriarchy and Accumulation on a World Scale*, ==1986==) described **"housewifisation"**: women's work everywhere — in homes, fields and export factories of the global South — is treated as secondary and paid little, which makes it profitable.
- **Sylvia Walby** (*Theorizing Patriarchy*, ==1990==) identified **six structures** of patriarchy: paid work, household production, culture, sexuality, violence and the state.

::: define Socialist feminism | the school of two systems
The view that women are oppressed by **capitalism and patriarchy working together**. It studies both **paid work** and **unpaid domestic work**, both **class** and **gender**, and seeks change in the **economy and the family** at the same time.
-> Key words: dual systems, capitalist patriarchy, family wage, double burden, social reproduction.
:::

## 4.5  Marxist and socialist feminism compared

::: fig compare | Marxist feminism and socialist feminism side by side.
## Marxist feminism
- Main enemy: capitalism and private property
- Gender oppression is a form of class oppression
- End capitalism, and women will be freed
- Engels, Bebel, Zetkin, Kollontai
## Socialist feminism
- Two enemies: capitalism and patriarchy
- Gender and class are equally basic
- Change the economy and the family together
- Mitchell, Hartmann, Eisenstein, Young
:::

::: table | Marxist and socialist feminism on the four questions.
@widths 2.4,5.8,5.8
Question | Marxist | Socialist
Cause | Capitalism and private property | Capitalism **and** patriarchy together
Where | The economy; class relations | The economy **and** the family, sexuality, the state
Remedy | Socialist revolution; women in paid work; socialised housework | Change both systems: paid care, shared housework, workers' and women's rights
Method | Class struggle; trade unions; socialist parties | Alliances of women's and workers' movements; campaigns on care and pay
:::

## 4.6  Stance on gender and exploitation — the key points

The 2021 question asked for the "philosophical stance" of the school. It can be summarised in six points.

1. **Materialism:** women's position is explained by **material** (economic) conditions, not by nature or ideas alone.
2. **Two kinds of exploitation:** women are exploited as **paid workers** (low wages, insecure jobs) and as **unpaid workers** (housework and care).
3. **The double burden:** women who take paid work still do the housework — a "**second shift**" (a phrase made famous by sociologist **Arlie Hochschild**, ==1989==).
4. **The family serves capital:** it produces and maintains workers cheaply and passes on property.
5. **Reserve army:** women are drawn into work in booms or wars and pushed out in slumps.
6. **Liberation requires economic change:** legal equality is not enough without economic independence and **socialised care** (public childcare, paid leave).

## 4.7  Strengths and criticisms

::: table | The case for and against Marxist and socialist feminism.
@widths 7,7
Strengths | Criticisms
Makes **unpaid care work** visible and measurable | Marxism is "**sex-blind**": it cannot explain male power within the working class (Hartmann)
Links gender to **class**: poor women's problems differ from rich women's | Reduces gender to economics; **violence and sexuality** are underplayed
Explains the **double burden** and low-paid "women's jobs" | Socialist states did not free women: Soviet women carried a **double burden**
Global reach: export factories, migrant care workers | Wages for housework could **fix women in the home**
Influences policy: time-use surveys, care economy, SDG target 5.4 | Dual-systems theory struggles to say how the two systems connect
:::

## 4.8  Marxist and socialist feminism in Pakistan

This school fits Pakistan's economic realities closely.

- **Unpaid family work.** Most employed women in Pakistan work in **agriculture**, much of it as **unpaid family helpers** (Labour Force Survey). Their labour on crops and livestock is often not counted as "work".
- **Home-based workers.** Millions of women stitch, embroider, make bangles or pack goods at home for piece rates. **Sindh** passed Pakistan's first **Home-Based Workers Act** in ==2018==.
- **Lady Health Workers.** The national **Lady Health Worker Programme** (==1994==) employs tens of thousands of women. Their long campaign for regular jobs and fair pay — including protests in Islamabad and a Supreme Court order of ==2012== to regularise them — is a classic socialist-feminist struggle over women's paid care work.
- **Peasant women's movements.** The **Sindhiani Tehreek** (from ==1982==) organised rural Sindhi women against military rule and feudal power. In the **Okara** military farms dispute (from ==2000==), women tenants' groups defended their land with sticks and wooden beaters.
- **Social protection.** The **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==) pays cash to **women** in poor households — recognising women's role in family survival and giving them some money of their own.
- **Left-wing feminist groups** such as the **Women Democratic Front** (founded ==2018==) link women's rights to workers' rights and are active in the Aurat March.

::: pakistan | The double burden in one Pakistani life
A woman in rural Punjab rises before dawn to milk the buffalo, cooks for a joint family, works in the cotton fields in picking season for low wages, collects water and fuel, and cares for children and in-laws. The survey may record her as "not in the labour force". Marxist and socialist feminism give you the words for her situation: **unpaid labour**, **social reproduction**, **double burden** and **reserve army**.
:::

::: eye | The model structure for 2021
1. Introduction: definition from the question's own words; Engels's thesis. 2. The Marxist background (five terms). 3. Development — classical (Engels, Bebel, Zetkin, Kollontai). 4. The domestic-labour debate (Benston, Dalla Costa and James, Federici). 5. The turn to socialist feminism (Mitchell, Hartmann, Eisenstein, Young). 6. The philosophical stance — six points. 7. Exploitation: paid, unpaid, double burden. 8. Criticisms. 9. Pakistan. 10. Conclusion. Draw the cycle of unpaid work, and the Marxist/socialist comparison.
:::
